function lesson(name, beginner, mechanism, example, deepDive, interviewAnswer, question, answer, sources) {
  return [name, { beginner, mechanism, example, deepDive, interviewAnswer, followUp: { question, answer }, sources }];
}

export const SYSTEM_DESIGN_CONCEPT_LESSONS = Object.fromEntries([
  lesson('Scalability',
    'Scalability means serving more work while keeping promises about response time, correctness and cost. A system that accepts more requests but makes most users wait longer has not necessarily scaled successfully. Specify which workload is growing: users, writes, stored data, or expensive computations.',
    'Vertical scaling gives one machine more resources. Horizontal scaling distributes work across machines. Stateless application instances are comparatively easy to replicate; shared databases, locks and hot keys can remain bottlenecks. Measure the saturated resource before choosing the scaling mechanism.',
    'Assume a product grows from 500 to 5,000 reads per second with a target that 95% of reads finish within 200 milliseconds (p95). Load-test representative requests, inspect database time, and compare a query improvement, cache, and additional instances. These figures are teaching assumptions, not promised capacity.',
    'More workers can increase database connections and worsen contention. A popular key may overload one partition even when total capacity looks healthy. Test skew, bursts and one-instance failure; compare successful throughput, 99th-percentile (p99) latency and cost per successful request rather than instance count.',
    'I define scalability against a workload and a measurable service target. I first find the limiting resource under representative peak load. I might add stateless instances for application CPU, improve queries for database work, or partition ownership when a single writer is the limit. Each choice adds costs or coordination. I verify improvement with successful throughput, tail latency and failure tests; adding servers alone is not evidence of scaling.',
    'Why not add more application instances immediately?', 'They help only if application resources are the bottleneck. If database locks or a hot partition dominate, extra instances can multiply contention. Compare resource saturation and per-stage timings before and after the change.', ['overload']),
  lesson('Availability',
    'Availability describes whether users can obtain a useful result when they need it. A process being alive is not enough: a checkout endpoint returning errors because its database is unavailable is not providing checkout service. Define success separately for each important operation.',
    'Measure successful eligible requests divided by eligible requests, or usable time within an agreed window. State which requests qualify, which outcomes count as success, and where measurement occurs. Replicas and failover can reduce some failures but also introduce dependencies and coordination.',
    'If 1,000,000 eligible requests include 1,000 failures, request-based availability is 99.9%. A recommendation failure might still allow a successful checkout; a missing payment confirmation cannot simply be counted as a successful purchase. Report the affected user journey.',
    'Request-based and time-based measurements answer different questions. A quiet five-minute outage can barely affect request totals. Regional averages can hide one tenant losing service. Test dependency outages and failover, separate durability from availability, and avoid assuming independent failures when calculating redundancy benefits.',
    'I define availability as the ability to complete a specific user operation successfully over a stated window. I select a request-based or time-based indicator and make exclusions explicit. I reduce important failure paths with redundancy, bounded dependencies and safe degradation, then test failover. I measure the result from the user boundary because healthy servers do not prove that users can complete the operation.',
    'Does a 200 response always count as available?', 'No. A syntactically successful response with missing required data or an incorrect result can violate the user contract. Define successful semantics and use checks capable of detecting that failure.', ['slo']),
  lesson('Latency',
    'Latency is the time one operation takes from the user’s perspective. It includes waiting as well as execution: network travel, queues, application work and downstream calls. Fast averages can coexist with a small group of users experiencing very slow requests.',
    'Percentiles describe a distribution: a p95 of 200 ms means approximately 95% of observed requests completed within that value in the measured population and window. Break end-to-end time into stages with traces, and distinguish client time from server processing time.',
    'Assume a search request spends 30 ms on the network, 20 ms queued, 80 ms in a database and 20 ms elsewhere: the sequential path totals 150 ms. Optimizing a 2 ms helper cannot remove the 80 ms database wait.',
    'For parallel calls, the request waits for the branches it actually needs; slow branches can dominate the tail. Percentiles cannot generally be added across services. Include timeouts and failed requests in separate outcome measurements so dropping slow work does not produce a misleading latency improvement.',
    'Latency is elapsed time for an individual operation, including queues and dependencies. I define the measurement boundary and examine p50, p95 and p99 alongside errors. Traces identify which stage contributes most, while load tests reveal queue growth. I optimize the critical path, bound waiting and check that faster responses still contain correct results. I would not claim success from a lower average alone.',
    'Why can latency worsen after adding concurrency?', 'More work can compete for the same CPU, connection pool or database locks. Once a shared resource saturates, requests queue. Increasing concurrency can raise waiting time without raising useful completion rate.', ['monitoring']),
  lesson('Throughput',
    'Throughput is completed work per unit time, such as successful orders per second or processed records per minute. It differs from incoming traffic and from latency. A server can receive many requests while successfully completing very few.',
    'Concurrency is work in progress; throughput is the rate at which work finishes. In a stable system, average work in progress equals average completion rate multiplied by average time in the system. Use consistent boundaries and units; an ever-growing queue is not a stable system.',
    'If a stable stage completes 100 requests per second and each spends an average 0.2 seconds there, it has about 20 requests in progress on average. This is a sizing illustration, not a rule that increasing the pool to 20 guarantees that throughput.',
    'Batching can increase throughput while making the first item wait for a batch. Retries, duplicates and failed work inflate raw processing counts. Test representative record sizes, dependencies and sustained load; track successful unique completions, queue age, and resource utilization together.',
    'Throughput measures useful work completed per time unit. I separate offered traffic, accepted work and successful completions, and define whether duplicates count. I use concurrency and service time to understand capacity, then test the actual bottleneck. Batching or parallelism may increase completion rate but also latency or contention, so I report throughput together with response-time distributions, errors and backlog growth.',
    'Can high throughput still mean poor user experience?', 'Yes. A batch can complete thousands of records per second while an individual user waits minutes. Specify both a completion-rate requirement and an end-to-end time target for the user operation.', ['overload']),
  lesson('SLO',
    'A service-level objective, or SLO, states how reliably a user outcome should be delivered. It turns “the service should be fast” into an explicit target. The measured signal is the service-level indicator, or SLI; the objective is the target for that signal.',
    'Define a good event, eligible events and a measurement window. For example, measure the fraction of eligible reads that return correct data within 300 ms. An agreement with customers that specifies consequences is an SLA; it is related to, but different from, an engineering objective.',
    'Assume an objective of 99.9% good reads over 30 days. A read that returns in 50 ms with the wrong tenant’s record is not good. Instrument the user boundary, identify expected traffic classes, and document how timeouts and dependency failures are counted.',
    'A global ratio can hide regional or customer-specific harm. Tail latency, correctness and freshness may need separate indicators. Choose achievable targets from user needs and dependency behavior, rather than choosing many nines because they sound impressive. Review the target when product expectations change.',
    'An SLO is a measurable reliability target for a user journey over a defined window. I specify eligible events and what makes one good, including latency and correctness where appropriate. The SLI provides the observed measurement; the SLO sets its target. I use the remaining failure allowance to guide operational decisions and keep any contractual SLA consequences distinct from the internal target.',
    'Is CPU utilization an SLO?', 'Usually it is a diagnostic or capacity signal. Users care about completing their operation. CPU can help explain an SLO miss, but a CPU threshold alone does not establish whether the user outcome succeeded.', ['slo']),
  lesson('Error budget',
    'An error budget is the amount of unreliability allowed by a service-level objective (SLO) during its window. It gives a team a shared basis for deciding when to prioritize reliability work. It is not permission to ignore users until a quota of failures has been consumed.',
    'For an event-based target, the allowed bad-event fraction is one minus the objective. With a 99.9% target and one million eligible events, the allowance is 1,000 bad events. Burn rate compares the observed failure fraction with the allowed fraction over a specified interval.',
    'At that target, a sustained 1% bad-event rate burns the budget ten times faster than the baseline allowance. Investigate impact and consider pausing risky rollout, rather than waiting for the monthly chart to turn red. Exact action thresholds belong to the service’s policy.',
    'Short windows detect sudden harm; longer windows distinguish sustained degradation from brief noise. Low traffic makes ratios unstable, so inspect event counts. Request-based budgets are not automatically equivalent to minutes of downtime. Define exclusions and policy beforehand to avoid negotiating metrics during an incident.',
    'The error budget translates an SLO into a bounded allowance for failed user outcomes. I measure consumption and burn rate over multiple windows, then apply an agreed policy to releases and reliability work. For example, unexpectedly rapid burn may stop a canary. The goal is to balance delivery with user impact, using explicit definitions rather than arguing about whether a service feels reliable.',
    'Should every release stop after one failure?', 'No. Decisions depend on impact, current burn, remaining allowance and the proposed change. A tiny isolated failure and a fast-growing regional outage need different responses; the policy should make that distinction explicit.', ['slo', 'canary']),
  lesson('Capacity planning',
    'Capacity planning estimates resources required for expected demand and failures. Start with user actions, request sizes, retention and peak patterns rather than selecting a machine type first. A useful estimate makes assumptions visible and identifies which measurements would change the design.',
    'Convert daily activity into average rates, then model peaks, read/write mix and per-request cost. Estimate storage growth, copies and network transfer separately. Reserve capacity for recovery and deployment overlap. Validate the model with representative load rather than treating arithmetic as a benchmark.',
    'Assume 8.64 million writes per day: that is 100 writes per second on average. A five-times peak assumption gives 500 writes per second. At 1 KB (1,000 bytes) per record, raw daily data is about 8.64 GB before indexes, replicas, metadata and retention cleanup.',
    'Traffic skew and expensive queries can invalidate average-based estimates. Scaling may take minutes, while a burst arrives in seconds. Test one-zone loss, backlog catch-up and storage exhaustion. Track forecast error and the resource that actually saturates; headroom should reflect measured uncertainty and recovery needs.',
    'I translate the workload into peak request rates, data growth, network traffic and resource demand, stating units and assumptions. I include indexes, replicas, retention and the capacity needed during failure or recovery. I then load-test realistic requests and skew to calibrate the estimate. Capacity planning is an evolving model, not proof that a particular instance count will meet the latency target.',
    'Why is average daily traffic insufficient?', 'An average hides bursts, hot customers and unequal request costs. The system must handle the relevant peak while preserving its contract, or explicitly queue or reject work within a bounded policy.', ['overload', 'cost']),
  lesson('Load balancer',
    'A load balancer distributes incoming traffic among eligible backends. It can route around an unhealthy instance and make several application servers appear behind one service endpoint. It does not make a slow database faster or automatically make the application stateless.',
    'Layer-four balancing routes transport connections; layer-seven balancing can inspect HTTP properties such as host or path. Policies include round-robin, least-connections and weighted routing. Health checks determine eligibility, while draining lets an instance stop receiving new work before shutdown.',
    'For three stateless API instances, distribute requests while excluding an instance that fails readiness. During a release, remove one from new traffic, allow its in-flight requests a bounded drain interval, and verify errors and latency before bringing replacements into service.',
    'Equal request counts need not mean equal work. Sticky sessions can concentrate users, and health checks can flap. Retries at the balancer may duplicate unsafe writes. Test slow backends, connection reuse and partial failure; preserve trusted client identity without accepting spoofed forwarding headers.',
    'A load balancer routes traffic to healthy, eligible service instances using a policy appropriate to the protocol and workload. I choose connection-level or HTTP-aware routing, define readiness and draining, and inspect how retries and sessions behave. It helps with distribution and instance failure, but shared dependencies and hot traffic can still limit the system. I verify balanced resource use and user-visible success during instance removal.',
    'Does round-robin guarantee balanced CPU?', 'No. Requests can have very different costs and connections can carry different traffic volumes. Compare backend utilization and latency, then choose a policy that fits the workload rather than assuming equal counts mean equal load.', ['balancing']),
  lesson('CDN',
    'A content delivery network, or CDN, serves cacheable content from distributed edge locations closer to users. It reduces repeated origin reads and network distance. It is especially useful for versioned static assets; personalized or sensitive responses require carefully scoped caching rules.',
    'An edge computes a cache key and checks whether a stored response is usable. A miss reaches the origin; cache directives control reuse and validation. Versioned URLs can make new assets available without overwriting cached old bytes. Cache keys must distinguish all response-changing dimensions.',
    'Publish app.4f2.js with a long cache lifetime because its URL changes with its contents. Keep account-specific JSON private or deliberately keyed and authorized. Compare origin request volume and latency from several regions; a high hit ratio is useful only if the returned content is correct.',
    'A missing tenant or authorization dimension in a shared cache can leak data. Invalidation is not necessarily instantaneous everywhere. Plan origin protection for cold caches and outages, and test query parameters, cookies and content negotiation. Never assume the CDN replaces application authorization.',
    'A CDN places reusable content near users and reduces origin traffic. I define which responses are public, their cache keys, freshness rules and invalidation strategy. Versioned static assets are straightforward; personalized responses require stricter isolation. I validate regional latency, origin load and correct variants, including cold-cache behavior. Cache efficiency must not come at the expense of freshness or tenant boundaries.',
    'Why can a CDN return another user’s data?', 'If a response depends on identity but the shared cache key does not, one user’s response can be reused for another. Prevent shared caching of sensitive responses unless authorization and variant isolation are explicitly designed and tested.', ['httpCache', 'authz']),
  lesson('DNS',
    'The Domain Name System, or DNS, resolves names such as api.example.com into records clients use to locate services. It separates a stable service name from changing endpoints. A successful lookup does not prove the target service is reachable or authorized.',
    'A resolver follows delegated authority and caches records for a time-to-live, or TTL. Different clients and recursive resolvers can therefore temporarily see different answers after a change. Applications may also hold existing network connections beyond a record’s cache lifetime.',
    'Before moving an API, lower its DNS TTL sufficiently ahead of the change, provision the new endpoint and keep the old one serving during the transition. Observe requests reaching both destinations rather than assuming every client switches when the authoritative record changes.',
    'Low TTL values increase lookup load and do not force active connections to move. Negative answers can also be cached. DNS-based failover depends on detection, authoritative updates, caches and client behavior, so it is not a precise per-request traffic switch. Test resolver and connection behavior during migration.',
    'DNS maps service names to records and relies on distributed caching. I use it for naming and coarse endpoint changes, with explicit TTL and overlap plans. For migration or failover I account for cached answers, existing connections and negative caching. I verify traffic and client errors at both endpoints. A DNS update is a routing input, not proof that every client has moved or that recovery is complete.',
    'Can a 30-second TTL guarantee a 30-second failover?', 'No. Detection and update time also matter, clients can reuse established connections, and resolver behavior varies. Measure the actual client population and design an overlap or fallback path instead of promising a universal deadline.', ['dns']),
  lesson('Relational database',
    'A relational database stores structured records in tables and supports relationships and constraints between them. It is useful when correctness depends on several related records, such as an order, its items and inventory reservations. SQL is the query language; the database also supplies transaction and storage behavior.',
    'Primary keys identify rows, foreign keys constrain references, and transactions group related changes. Indexes support access paths. Isolation controls what concurrent transactions observe; it does not automatically make every business rule safe. Design queries and constraints around the operations the application must perform.',
    'For an order, insert its header and items together, check available inventory with appropriate concurrency control, and commit only when the local invariant holds. Test two customers buying the last item. Merely wrapping separate read and write statements in a transaction may still allow a race.',
    'Read replicas, partitioning and connection pooling address different limits. Joining everything into one huge query can be as harmful as issuing a query per row. Check execution plans, lock waits and transaction duration. Treat deadlocks or serialization failures as classified outcomes with bounded whole-transaction retry where safe.',
    'I choose a relational database when structured relationships, transactional changes and enforceable constraints fit the domain. I model the important invariants with keys, constraints and an appropriate isolation strategy, then design indexes for actual queries. I validate concurrency and failure behavior rather than assuming SQL implies correctness. Relational systems can scale, but the approach depends on query shape, write ownership and operational constraints.',
    'Does a transaction prevent overselling by itself?', 'Not necessarily. Two transactions can both read an available item under some isolation patterns. Use an atomic conditional update, locking or stronger isolation as appropriate, and test that exactly one competing purchase succeeds.', ['transactions', 'indexes']),
  lesson('NoSQL database',
    'NoSQL describes several database families, including key-value, document, wide-column and graph stores. They make different choices about data shape, queries and distribution. The label does not imply that every such database has the same consistency, transaction support or scaling behavior.',
    'Start with access patterns and ownership. A document might colocate data commonly read together; a key-value design supports direct key access. Secondary indexes, cross-record transactions and consistency options are product-specific. Denormalization moves some relationship maintenance from query execution into write and update workflows.',
    'A shopping-cart document can group a customer’s current items for one-key reads. If product prices change independently, decide whether the cart stores a historical quote or refreshes from the price authority. Test concurrent item edits and large carts instead of assuming one document solves all coordination.',
    'Duplicated fields can diverge, hot partition keys can concentrate load, and ad hoc queries may require scans or new projections. Partitioned systems still have correctness constraints. Compare concrete products and configuration against required reads, writes, conflict handling and recovery; avoid a blanket SQL-versus-NoSQL performance claim.',
    'I select a NoSQL family for a specific data model and access pattern, not because of its label. I identify the partition key, required consistency, transaction boundary and update strategy for duplicated data. A document or key-value store can simplify a well-defined workload, but cross-entity invariants and evolving queries may be harder. I prove the fit with representative queries, concurrent writes and failure tests.',
    'Are all NoSQL reads eventually consistent?', 'No. Consistency and transaction guarantees differ across products, operations and configurations. Name the actual system and required guarantee, then test the documented read/write path rather than inferring behavior from the NoSQL category.', ['documents', 'readConsistency']),
  lesson('ACID transaction',
    'ACID names four transaction properties: atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability. Atomicity makes the protected changes commit together or roll back. Consistency means valid transitions preserve declared rules; isolation concerns concurrent execution; durability concerns retaining acknowledged commits under the database’s stated failure assumptions.',
    'A transaction manager coordinates commit and rollback with storage and concurrency control. Constraints enforce some rules, while application logic must express others correctly. The isolation level determines allowed observations. External actions, such as sending an email or charging a provider, are not automatically rolled back with the database.',
    'Transfer 10 units from one local account row to another in one transaction. A constraint can reject negative balances, while locking or an atomic update protects concurrent debits. Crash before commit and verify no partial transfer; crash after acknowledged commit and verify the configured durability behavior.',
    'A transaction is not an assurance of serial execution at every isolation level. Write skew, where concurrent decisions jointly violate a rule, and lost updates, where one writer overwrites another’s change, require explicit analysis. A committed payment plus a failed response is an ambiguous caller outcome, not evidence of rollback. Two-phase commit exists for some distributed participants but carries coordination and operational costs.',
    'ACID describes atomic commit, preservation of declared invariants, concurrent-observation rules and durability. I specify the database and isolation level, then test the business invariant with competing transactions and crashes. I also identify effects outside the transaction boundary. A local rollback cannot undo an external charge, so I need idempotency, an outbox or a coordinated workflow rather than assuming one transaction covers every service.',
    'Is ACID consistency the same as replica consistency?', 'No. ACID consistency concerns valid database states and invariants. Replica consistency describes what reads can observe across copies and time. A transaction can satisfy its local constraints while a replica still returns an older committed value.', ['transactions', 'twoPhase']),
  lesson('Index',
    'An index is an extra access structure that helps a database find selected rows without examining every row. It trades additional storage and maintenance on writes for faster access on suitable queries. An index is useful because of a workload, not simply because a column exists.',
    'Different index types support different operators and ordering. A composite index’s column order affects which filters and sorts it can support. The query planner estimates costs using statistics. Fetching many scattered rows through an index can be more expensive than a sequential scan.',
    'For “latest orders for one customer,” consider an index aligned with customer_id and created_at, then inspect the actual execution plan and timing. Include realistic customer sizes and pagination. Compare write throughput and storage as well as read latency before accepting the change.',
    'Low selectivity, stale statistics, implicit conversions and incompatible predicates can prevent the expected access path. Additional indexes increase write amplification and maintenance. A uniqueness index can enforce a business rule, but null semantics and online creation behavior depend on the database and definition.',
    'I add an index to support a known filter, join or ordering pattern. I choose its columns and type from the query and data distribution, then inspect the plan and representative execution results. The benefit must outweigh storage and write costs. I also test skew and parameter variation because an index that helps one selective query may not help a broad scan.',
    'Why might a database ignore an index?', 'The planner may estimate that too many rows match, the predicate may not match the index’s operators or leading columns, or statistics may be inaccurate. Compare the plan and actual row counts before forcing a particular path.', ['indexes']),
  lesson('Replication',
    'Replication maintains copies of data on other nodes. Copies can support read traffic or recovery after a node fails, depending on the replication design. Replication is different from a backup: an accidental deletion can be copied just as effectively as a valid update.',
    'A primary may send a log of changes to replicas. Asynchronous replication can acknowledge a write before a replica receives it; synchronous configurations wait for specified acknowledgements. Define whether acknowledgements mean received, persisted or applied, and which node can legitimately accept writes.',
    'After updating a profile on the primary, an immediate read from a lagging replica may show the old name. For a read-your-write requirement, route the read to the authority or use a supported position-aware mechanism. Measure replication lag and recovery behavior under load.',
    'Promotion can lose unreplicated writes or create competing writers unless ownership is controlled. Synchronous replication can increase latency or reduce write availability when required peers are unavailable. Test failover with network partitions, fence the old writer and verify data continuity before directing traffic to the new authority.',
    'Replication creates copies for read capacity or failure recovery, with explicit lag and acknowledgement semantics. I choose synchronous or asynchronous behavior from the durability, latency and availability requirements. I define read freshness and promotion ownership, then test lag, partitions and failover. Replicas do not replace backups, and a failover is successful only if the resulting data and writer authority satisfy the service contract.',
    'Can a replica always serve a fresh read?', 'No. An asynchronous replica can lag behind committed writes. Even synchronous acknowledgement does not always mean the change is already applied for reads. Use the specific replication guarantees and a read path that meets the required freshness.', ['replicas']),
  lesson('Sharding',
    'Sharding divides data and its workload among independently owned partitions, often on different nodes. It is useful when one ownership boundary cannot meet capacity needs. It differs from keeping replicated copies of the same data, and from merely partitioning one table on one server.',
    'A routing rule maps a stable key to a shard. Writes and local transactions go to that owner. Range, hash and directory-based approaches make different trade-offs in locality, balance and movement. Global queries and cross-shard invariants need additional coordination or explicit workflow boundaries.',
    'Assign each tenant’s orders to one owning shard so tenant-local operations stay together. Measure the largest tenant separately: hashing many tenant IDs evenly does not divide one huge tenant. Document how routing changes while a shard’s data is copied and caught up.',
    'Resharding needs a snapshot plus subsequent changes, an ownership cutover and a way to prevent stale routers writing the old owner. Cross-shard joins and uniqueness can become expensive. Verify row counts, checksums or domain invariants, and rehearse a partial movement failure before production migration.',
    'I shard when a single data owner is a measured limit and the workload has a defensible partition boundary. I choose the key for both load distribution and transaction locality, then design routing, rebalancing and cross-shard operations. Sharding adds operational and correctness complexity. I validate hot-key behavior and ownership transfer, not just whether rows appear evenly distributed.',
    'Does adding shards fix a hot key?', 'Not if all traffic for that key still reaches one owner. Splitting that workload may require a different data model, subpartitioning, aggregation or admission control, and can change ordering or transaction guarantees.', ['sharding', 'partitions']),
  lesson('Partition key',
    'A partition key determines which partition owns a record or message. It influences load balance, which operations can remain local, and often the scope of ordering. A key is therefore part of the correctness and growth design, not just a hashing detail.',
    'A router applies the key to a placement rule such as a hash or directory. Related records may be colocated so one owner can enforce an invariant. High key cardinality can help distribution, but actual traffic skew matters more than the number of distinct values.',
    'Using order_id for events can keep one order’s updates together. Using a constant country code for every request from a large market can create a hot partition. Test the busiest keys and verify that updates needing local ordering use a compatible key.',
    'A key that spreads writes well can make a required query scatter across every partition. Changing the key affects stored placement, routing, deduplication and possibly ordering. For multi-tenant data, tenant identity must also be part of authorization; a routing key alone is not an access control.',
    'I choose a partition key by balancing traffic distribution with locality for important queries and invariants. I examine skew and growth, not just whether the key has many possible values. I also state the resulting ordering and transaction boundaries. If the key later changes, I treat that as a data migration with explicit routing ownership and validation.',
    'Why not always use a random identifier?', 'It may spread load well but separate records that must be queried or changed together. A good key serves the important access patterns and invariants while avoiding unacceptable hot partitions.', ['sharding', 'kafka']),
  lesson('Consistency model',
    'A consistency model states what values and ordering a reader is allowed to observe after writes. It is separate from whether a request succeeds and from whether a transaction preserves local constraints. Different operations in one product can need different guarantees.',
    'Read-your-writes lets a client observe its own successful update; eventual convergence permits temporary differences between copies under the system’s assumptions. Linearizable operations behave as if they take effect at one point between invocation and response. Name the required guarantee instead of using “strong” without explanation.',
    'A profile page may require a user to see their new name immediately, while an aggregate analytics dashboard can tolerate a stated delay. Inventory reservation may need one authoritative conditional decision. Write tests around these user outcomes rather than selecting one slogan for the whole application.',
    'Replication lag, concurrent writes and network partitions expose the trade-offs. Stronger coordination can increase latency and reduce availability for operations that cannot reach the required participants. Eventual consistency also needs conflict and convergence rules; it does not mean that arbitrary incorrect states will repair themselves.',
    'I define consistency per operation: what a reader may observe after an acknowledged write and how concurrent updates are ordered. A user’s own profile edit may need read-your-writes, whereas analytics can accept bounded staleness. I choose the storage and routing mechanism for that guarantee, then test concurrent writes, lag and partitions. Stronger coordination and weaker freshness each have concrete costs.',
    'Is eventual consistency the same as a known freshness bound?', 'No. Eventual convergence alone does not promise a maximum delay. If the product requires data no more than five seconds old, define, measure and enforce that additional bound, including what happens when it cannot be met.', ['apiGuarantees', 'readConsistency']),
  lesson('Cache',
    'A cache stores a reusable copy of data or computation to reduce repeated work. The original authority still decides the truth unless a different ownership model is explicitly designed. A cache needs a rule for when a stored value is safe to reuse.',
    'In cache-aside, the application looks up a key, reads the authority on a miss and stores the result. A time-to-live limits reuse duration, while eviction controls memory pressure. Keys must include dimensions that change the result, such as tenant, query and representation version.',
    'Cache public product descriptions for a stated freshness interval, while authorizing account-specific reads before using an appropriately scoped key. Compare source load and latency with cold and warm caches. Delete or update the cache after a description change under the chosen consistency policy.',
    'Concurrent misses can stampede the source, and a slow stale fill can overwrite a newer value. Bound concurrent loads, consider version checks or coalescing, and plan for total cache loss. Cache only failures that are safe to reuse; an authorization failure must not become a cross-user response.',
    'I use a cache to reduce repeated work under an explicit freshness and isolation contract. I define the authority, cache key, expiry, invalidation and miss behavior. I test concurrent misses, stale fills and cache outages, then measure origin load, hit ratio and correct response latency. A high hit ratio is not success if the cached data is stale beyond the promise or belongs to another tenant.',
    'Does a five-minute TTL prevent every stale read?', 'No. It permits reuse during that interval, and races or missing invalidations can still return an older value. Define the acceptable freshness and protect update/fill ordering where stricter behavior is required.', ['cache', 'httpCache']),
  lesson('Cache invalidation',
    'Cache invalidation stops an outdated copy from being treated as current after authoritative data changes. Expiry removes a value after time; invalidation responds to a known change. The design must also cover concurrent readers and writers, not just the simple update-then-delete sequence.',
    'A write can update or evict relevant keys after committing the authoritative change. Event-driven invalidation must handle delivery lag and duplicates. Versioned values or generation checks can reject stale fills. The correct mechanism depends on whether the product permits a stale interval.',
    'Reader A starts fetching version 7. Writer B commits version 8 and evicts the cache. If A then stores version 7, the old value returns. A version-aware fill can reject that write; alternatively, use a design whose explicit freshness contract tolerates the remaining race.',
    'Deleting every key is expensive and can trigger a miss storm. Derived views may depend on several records, making dependency tracking necessary. Test out-of-order invalidations, missed events, delete-and-recreate cycles and cache restart. A timestamp comparison is unsafe if its ordering assumptions do not hold.',
    'I design invalidation around the write authority and the allowed staleness. After a committed change, I evict, update or version the affected copies, and analyze stale-fill races. For distributed invalidation I assume events can be delayed or repeated and provide a repair path. I verify behavior with interleaved reads and writes because a correct single-threaded sequence does not prove cache consistency.',
    'Why is deleting after commit not always sufficient?', 'A reader that fetched old data before the commit may populate the cache after deletion. Protect the fill with an authoritative version or generation, or explicitly accept and bound the resulting stale interval.', ['httpCache', 'outbox']),
  lesson('Message queue',
    'A message queue holds work between a producer and a consumer so they do not have to run at the same speed or at the same moment. Durability, ordering and redelivery depend on the broker and configuration; merely sending a message does not prove the business task finished.',
    'The producer publishes, the broker accepts under its durability policy, a consumer receives, and an acknowledgement advances or removes the delivery. If processing succeeds but acknowledgement is lost, work may be delivered again. Admission limits and retention prevent the backlog from becoming an unbounded promise.',
    'Accept a video-transcoding job with an operation ID and return a job-status URL. Workers process it later and store a terminal result. If a worker crashes after writing the output, redelivery should recognize the same job rather than charge or publish twice.',
    'Backlog depth alone hides old stuck work; measure oldest-message age and completion time. Poison messages, large payloads and consumer downtime need explicit policies. Test crashes before and after the side effect and acknowledgement, and size retention for realistic outage and recovery durations.',
    'A queue decouples acceptance from later processing and absorbs bounded bursts. I define publish durability, acknowledgement, redelivery, ordering, retention and overload behavior. Each job has a durable identity and observable status, and consumers tolerate repeated delivery. I monitor queue age and successful completion, because accepting messages faster than they finish can conceal overload rather than solve it.',
    'Can a queue guarantee that an external charge happens once?', 'Not by itself. The consumer and payment boundary need a stable operation identity and deduplication or provider idempotency. Broker delivery guarantees do not automatically cover an unrelated external side effect.', ['kafka']),
  lesson('Idempotent consumer',
    'An idempotent consumer prevents repeated delivery of the same logical operation from repeating its business effect. Idempotency is about the intended effect, not necessarily identical timestamps, logs or network responses. The operation must have an identity that remains stable across legitimate retries.',
    'Store a processed-operation identity with the business state in one transaction where possible. A uniqueness constraint arbitrates concurrent duplicates. For an external provider, pass an idempotency key under that provider’s contract and persist the resulting outcome. A separate “seen” check followed by a write is racy.',
    'Two workers receive payment event P7 simultaneously. Both may start, but only one transaction should record P7 and apply the local ledger effect. If the provider charged before a crash, reconcile or retry with the same provider operation key rather than inventing a new payment.',
    'Deduplication retention must cover the replay horizon; expiring the record too early permits a later duplicate. Reusing one key with a different payload should be rejected or explicitly handled. Test concurrent delivery, commit ambiguity, replay after restart and the boundary between local state and external effects.',
    'I make the business effect idempotent using a durable operation identity and an atomic relationship between deduplication and state change. Concurrent duplicates are resolved by the owning store, not an in-memory check. For external effects I use the provider’s idempotency and reconciliation contract. I keep identities long enough for possible replay and test crashes around each commit boundary.',
    'Is setting a boolean processed flag enough?', 'Only if the flag and protected business effect change atomically under the correct identity and concurrency rules. Setting it before the effect can lose work; setting it afterward can allow duplicates after a crash.', ['kafka', 'outbox']),
  lesson('Delivery guarantee',
    'A delivery guarantee defines possible loss and repetition across a specific messaging boundary. At-most-once can lose a message without repeating delivery; at-least-once can repeat it. Exactly-once claims require a stated scope and do not automatically include every database or external provider.',
    'The guarantee depends on publishing acknowledgements, persisted broker state, consumer offsets and the transaction boundary for processing. Losing an acknowledgement can make success ambiguous. A consumer that commits an external effect separately from its offset must address the gap between those two commits.',
    'A worker inserts an invoice and crashes before committing its queue offset. At-least-once delivery can replay the message. A unique invoice-operation key can make that replay safe. Committing the offset first instead risks losing the invoice if the worker crashes before the insert.',
    'Some platforms provide transactions over their own records and offsets, but an unrelated payment API remains outside that scope. Configuration, replication and retention affect guarantees. Draw the exact failure boundary and test producer retry, broker recovery and consumer restart instead of accepting a broad “exactly once” label.',
    'I state delivery semantics for a named boundary and failure model. At-least-once delivery means the consumer must tolerate repeated messages; at-most-once trades repetition avoidance for possible loss. If exactly-once processing is required, I identify the transaction or deduplication boundary that provides it. I separately prove external business effects, since broker guarantees alone cannot establish what happened in another system.',
    'Why not always choose exactly once?', 'Its scope may exclude the actual side effect, and coordination can add latency, availability constraints or operational cost. First define the business invariant, then choose a mechanism whose documented boundary really covers it.', ['kafka']),
  lesson('Ordering',
    'Ordering defines which events must be observed before others. A useful requirement usually applies to one entity, such as an order’s lifecycle, rather than every event in the company. Global ordering can add coordination that unrelated operations do not need.',
    'A broker may order records within a partition. Consistent key routing keeps related events together, but parallel consumer work can still finish out of order. Sequence numbers or authoritative entity versions let a consumer reject stale updates, detect gaps and decide whether recovery is required.',
    'Order O1 emits version 4 “paid” followed by version 5 “shipped.” If version 4 arrives after version 5 through a retry path, a projection should not change the order back to paid. Apply the entity’s transition and version rules rather than overwriting blindly.',
    'Repartitioning, multiple producers and dead-letter replay can disrupt expected processing order. Waiting indefinitely for a missing event blocks progress. Define a bounded gap policy, replay or snapshot repair, and whether different entities may proceed independently. Test delayed, duplicated and reordered deliveries.',
    'I define ordering around the invariant that needs it, typically per entity or partition. I keep related events on a compatible routing key and preserve order through processing, or use versions to detect stale and missing updates. I also design recovery for gaps and replay. I avoid global ordering unless there is a concrete cross-entity rule that justifies its coordination cost.',
    'Does ordered delivery guarantee ordered completion?', 'No. A consumer can dispatch received records to parallel workers whose tasks finish in a different order. Preserve entity serialization or validate authoritative versions when applying the result.', ['kafka']),
  lesson('Transactional outbox',
    'A transactional outbox records the intention to publish an event in the same local database transaction as the business change. It solves the gap where data commits but a separate message send is lost. Publication still happens later, so downstream completion is asynchronous.',
    'Commit business rows and an outbox row together. A polling relay or change-data-capture process publishes the outbox record. A crash after publication but before recording relay progress can publish it again, so events need stable identities and consumers need appropriate deduplication.',
    'When creating an order, insert both the order and OrderCreated event into one transaction. If the broker is unavailable, the outbox remains for later relay. Expose a pending state if the user outcome depends on downstream work; do not claim that publication already completed.',
    'Keep event identities stable and define aggregate ordering, retention and relay ownership. A large old outbox indicates stalled propagation even if order writes succeed. Test rollback, relay restart, duplicate publish and schema evolution. An outbox does not atomically commit a third-party side effect with the local database.',
    'I use an outbox when a local state change must reliably lead to an event without a distributed transaction. The state and event intention commit together, then a relay publishes later. I expect duplicate publication around relay crashes and design consumer idempotency. I monitor the oldest unpublished event and define ordering and cleanup so the outbox remains an operable workflow rather than a hidden backlog.',
    'Does an outbox provide exactly-once publication?', 'Not automatically. The relay can publish and lose its acknowledgement or progress update. Stable event IDs and deduplication protect effects; stronger publication semantics require a specific coordination mechanism and clearly defined scope.', ['outbox']),
  lesson('Dead-letter queue',
    'A dead-letter queue holds messages that cannot safely continue through normal processing. It preserves evidence and enables controlled repair. Moving a message there is not the same as completing the business operation; someone still owns its unresolved outcome.',
    'Classify failures and apply bounded retry before quarantine where appropriate. Preserve the original operation ID, attempt history and safe diagnostic metadata. A replay procedure must decide whether the cause is fixed, whether an effect already occurred, and whether the message is still valid.',
    'A malformed shipping message repeatedly fails validation. Quarantine it, alert the owning team and keep the order visibly pending or failed. After correcting the source or transformation, replay with the same logical identity and verify that an earlier partial shipment is not duplicated.',
    'Replaying older messages can violate ordering or resurrect deleted state. Bulk replay can overload the repaired dependency. Protect sensitive payloads, limit access, define retention and replay rate, and track age and terminal outcomes. Treat a growing quarantine as user-impacting work rather than a clean main-queue metric.',
    'A dead-letter queue is a controlled holding path for work that cannot progress safely. I define which failures qualify, who investigates, what metadata is retained and how replay is authorized. Replay preserves operation identity and rechecks current state. I monitor age and unresolved user outcomes, and rate-limit recovery so clearing the dead-letter queue does not create another incident.',
    'Should every failure go directly to the dead-letter queue?', 'No. A transient timeout and a permanently invalid payload need different policies. Use bounded retries for retryable failures, quarantine when intervention is needed, and represent the business outcome honestly throughout.', ['kafka', 'retries']),
  lesson('Backpressure',
    'Backpressure communicates that downstream capacity cannot keep up with incoming work. The producer, intake or intermediary must slow, bound, defer or reject work. Simply adding a queue without a limit delays the symptom while memory use and completion time grow.',
    'Bound in-flight work, queue capacity and waiting time. Consumers may request only what they can process, or an API may apply admission limits. Define what the producer does when capacity is exhausted, including retry guidance and whether durable deferred acceptance is available.',
    'A worker pool can process 100 jobs per second while intake reaches 150. The backlog grows by roughly 50 jobs per second until behavior changes. Cap acceptance or add justified capacity, and expose job age; accepting indefinitely would promise work that cannot finish on time.',
    'Backpressure must propagate across hops without converting rejection into a retry storm. Shared limits may starve small tenants behind a large one, so consider fairness and workload cost. Test a slow dependency, consumer pause and recovery; watch oldest-job age, rejected work and successful completion.',
    'I use backpressure to keep work within finite capacity and a useful completion deadline. I bound concurrency and buffers, then define whether excess work is slowed, durably deferred or rejected. The signal must reach producers with a safe retry policy. I monitor backlog age and fairness because an apparently healthy intake can still be accepting work faster than the system can complete it.',
    'Why is a larger queue not always the fix?', 'It does not increase the processing rate. It can make users wait longer and increase recovery time. Choose a bounded queue from the completion-time requirement and adjust intake or processing capacity accordingly.', ['overload']),
  lesson('Retry policy',
    'A retry policy decides when and how to attempt an operation again after failure. Retrying can help with transient faults, but it adds traffic and may repeat a business effect. A timeout means the caller stopped waiting; it does not prove the operation failed to commit.',
    'Classify retryable outcomes, cap attempts and elapsed time, and use backoff (increasing waits) with jitter (random variation). Carry a stable operation identity for unsafe side effects and respect the remaining caller deadline. Prefer one deliberate retry owner to multiplying attempts independently at every layer.',
    'For a payment timeout, first use the provider’s operation identity to reconcile or safely repeat the same payment. A new payment ID could charge twice. If three layers each make three total attempts, one incoming operation can create up to 27 leaf attempts.',
    'Retries can keep an overloaded dependency overloaded. Include connection setup in timeout analysis, budget recovery traffic and stop on permanent validation or authorization failures. Test lost responses after commit, exhausted budgets and correlated outages. A circuit breaker or retry quota may be needed to contain pressure.',
    'I retry only classified transient failures within a finite attempt and time budget. I use backoff and jitter to avoid synchronized load, choose a clear retry owner, and preserve operation identity. For an ambiguous write I reconcile rather than assume failure. I verify that retries improve successful outcomes without multiplying overload or duplicating side effects, including the crash-after-commit case.',
    'Is exponential backoff enough to make a retry safe?', 'No. Backoff changes timing, not business semantics. Repeating a non-idempotent write can still duplicate its effect. The operation needs idempotency, reconciliation or a policy that forbids an unsafe retry.', ['retries']),
  lesson('Saga',
    'A saga coordinates a business workflow through multiple local transactions, with explicit compensation or forward recovery when a later step fails. Distributed transactions are not impossible; a saga is an alternative when one coordinated transaction is unavailable or unsuitable for the participants and workflow.',
    'An orchestrator can track steps centrally, or services can react through events. Persist workflow identity and state, make repeated commands safe, and define which transitions are allowed. Compensation is another business action, not a magical rollback of everything that happened.',
    'Reserve inventory, authorize payment and arrange shipment. If shipment cannot proceed, release the reservation and void or refund payment according to its actual state. A dispatched package cannot simply be “rolled back”; the workflow may require a return process or manual resolution.',
    'Other users can observe intermediate local commits, so business isolation needs explicit reservations or state rules. Compensation can also fail and must be retried safely or escalated. Test duplicate and reordered replies, timeouts after commit, restart and expired reservations before declaring the workflow correct.',
    'I use a saga for a multi-service workflow whose steps commit locally and cannot reasonably share one transaction boundary. I persist the workflow state, define idempotent step identities, and choose compensation or forward recovery for each failure. Intermediate state and failed compensation remain real concerns. I compare this with a supported distributed transaction when appropriate rather than claiming that cross-service coordination is inherently impossible.',
    'Can compensation always restore the original state?', 'No. An email may already be read and goods may already be shipped. Compensation is a domain-specific repair, such as a refund or return, with its own failure and authorization rules.', ['saga', 'twoPhase']),
  lesson('Batch processing',
    'Batch processing handles a bounded collection of work, often on a schedule or from a dataset, instead of answering one interactive request immediately. It emphasizes correct completion, restart and resource use. A batch can still have a deadline and user-visible freshness requirements.',
    'Partition the input, record checkpoints marking committed input progress, and define output commit boundaries. Retrying a chunk must not duplicate protected effects. Track which input version belongs to the run so a restart processes a coherent set rather than accidentally mixing yesterday’s and today’s data.',
    'Process a daily billing dataset in chunks of 1,000 records, using stable invoice identities and committed progress. Crash after one chunk, restart and verify that completed invoices are not issued again. Publish a final summary only after required validation succeeds.',
    'A checkpoint written before output can lose work; written afterward without idempotency it can repeat work. Skewed partitions create stragglers. Test partial output, corrupt records, input changes and reruns, and keep batch concurrency from starving interactive traffic. Define recovery and operator intervention for unprocessable records.',
    'I design a batch around a defined input, deadline and restart contract. I split work into manageable units, make effects safe to repeat, and coordinate output with checkpoints. I monitor progress, stragglers and completion correctness rather than only records per second. I also bound resource use and test failure between processing and checkpointing, since a fast batch that silently duplicates or skips work is incorrect.',
    'Is restarting the whole job always safe?', 'Only if its effects are idempotent or isolated until an atomic publication step. Emails, invoices and external calls may already have happened, so define replay identity and output ownership before relying on a full rerun.', ['kafka', 'outbox']),
  lesson('Timeout budget',
    'A timeout budget limits how long an operation may consume resources or make its caller wait. A complete request can include connection establishment, queueing and several dependencies. Giving every nested call the full original timeout can make the total exceed the user’s deadline.',
    'Choose an end-to-end deadline and pass only the remaining useful time to child work, reserving time for response and cleanup. Connection, read and total timeouts may cover different stages. Cancellation should release local resources, but it is not proof that remote work stopped.',
    'Assume a 500 ms request budget. If validation and queueing already consumed 100 ms, a downstream call cannot legitimately receive another full 500 ms. Reserve response time, constrain any retry to the remainder, and fail honestly when completing within the deadline is no longer possible.',
    'A payment may commit just before the caller times out. Classify this as an unknown outcome and reconcile using operation identity. Test slow connection setup, queued work, child cancellation and cleanup; a response timeout without resource release can leave the system overloaded after users have already left.',
    'I define one end-to-end budget and allocate remaining time across the critical path. I include queue and connection time, reserve cleanup time and stop work that no longer has a useful deadline. I propagate cancellation where supported while treating remote writes as potentially committed. I verify both user latency and resource release, and resolve ambiguous effects through identity and reconciliation.',
    'Does cancelling a request undo its database commit?', 'No. Cancellation and transaction outcome are separate. A commit may have completed before cancellation arrived. Query the authoritative operation state or use a safe idempotent replay protocol rather than telling the user it definitely failed.', ['retries']),
  lesson('Circuit breaker',
    'A circuit breaker temporarily prevents calls to a dependency that is failing badly. It protects local capacity and gives the dependency a chance to recover. It does not repair the dependency or decide whether a repeated business operation is safe.',
    'In a common model, closed allows calls, open rejects calls, and half-open permits limited recovery probes. Define measured failures, minimum sample volume, observation windows and recovery criteria. Use timeouts alongside the breaker so a permitted call still cannot wait indefinitely.',
    'If a recommendation dependency repeatedly times out, open its breaker and omit recommendations while checkout continues, provided recommendations are optional. Allow a small number of probes later. If payment is essential, fail or expose pending status rather than returning a fabricated success.',
    'One global breaker can let one noisy tenant block everyone, while too many tiny breakers have poor samples and high state cost. Probes can stampede after a common cooldown. Test state transitions, slow calls, recovery flapping and fallback load; choose scope and jitter deliberately.',
    'A circuit breaker stops repeatedly invoking a demonstrably unhealthy dependency. I define its failure signal, scope, sample window, open behavior and bounded recovery probes. It works with timeouts and capacity limits, and its fallback must preserve the business contract. I test opening, recovery and flapping because a breaker that never recovers or overloads its fallback simply moves the outage.',
    'Is a circuit breaker a substitute for retries?', 'No. A retry attempts recovery for one operation; a breaker controls whether calls should be attempted during broader dependency failure. Combine them carefully so permitted retries remain bounded and safe.', ['breaker']),
  lesson('Bulkhead',
    'A bulkhead separates resource capacity so one failing workload cannot consume everything shared by the application. The name comes from compartments that limit how far damage spreads. The protected resource might be threads, connections, queue slots or concurrent requests.',
    'Allocate independent bounded pools or permits to dependencies or traffic classes. Requests acquire capacity before starting and release it exactly once when the protected local work actually ends. A caller timeout must not release a permit while that protected work still occupies the resource. Bound any admission wait.',
    'Give a slow reporting integration a separate concurrency limit from payment processing. When reporting stalls, its limited capacity fills and new reporting work is rejected or deferred, while payment retains its own capacity. Test that connection pools and shared executors do not accidentally reconnect the two limits.',
    'Logical separation is ineffective if both paths still exhaust the same underlying database or CPU. Static partitions can waste capacity or unfairly starve tenants. Test exceptions, cancellation, timeout and double completion for permit leaks; measure saturation, wait time and successful service for the protected workload.',
    'I use a bulkhead to bound the resources one dependency or workload can consume. I separate the relevant pools or permits, define admission and ensure release on every terminal path. I then verify that hidden shared resources do not defeat the isolation. The trade-off is reserved capacity and utilization, but the benefit is keeping a noncritical failure from exhausting critical work.',
    'Why can separate thread pools still fail together?', 'Both may share a finite database connection pool, CPU quota or network dependency. Identify the actual constrained resources and isolate or limit them as well; a thread-pool label alone does not create full failure isolation.', ['bulkhead']),
  lesson('Load shedding',
    'Load shedding deliberately rejects or defers some work when capacity cannot serve everything usefully. It protects the work that remains admitted. The policy should follow business priority and fairness rather than letting memory exhaustion or random timeouts decide who loses service.',
    'Place admission checks before expensive work. Use signals such as concurrency saturation, queue age or remaining deadline, with per-customer limits where needed. Return an honest overload response and safe retry guidance. Deferred acceptance requires durable capacity and an explicit later-completion contract.',
    'During a burst, preserve checkout while disabling expensive nonessential report generation. Bound even critical work to what dependencies can sustain. Compare successful checkout completions and rejection rates; simply returning fast errors can improve latency charts while harming nearly every user.',
    'Rejection can itself be expensive, and aggressive clients can turn overload responses into more load. Choose cheap enforcement points and coordinate client backoff. Test priority inversion, tenant fairness and distinct overload/recovery thresholds so the system does not oscillate between accepting everything and rejecting everything.',
    'I shed load explicitly before finite resources collapse. I define priorities, fairness and admission signals, then reject or durably defer work that cannot meet its contract. Clients receive a response that does not encourage unsafe retry storms. I verify successful useful work, rejected demand and recovery behavior, rather than judging success by reduced CPU or faster errors alone.',
    'How does shedding differ from backpressure?', 'Backpressure is a capacity signal that should influence upstream behavior. Shedding is one possible response: deliberately decline work. A system may also slow producers or use bounded durable deferral, depending on the operation.', ['overload']),
  lesson('Health check',
    'A health check answers a specific operational question about an instance. Liveness commonly asks whether restarting might help; readiness asks whether it should receive work. Neither a running process nor an open port proves that a user operation can complete correctly.',
    'Define each check’s scope, timeout and failure threshold. Readiness can include capabilities required for the traffic routed to that instance. Avoid making liveness depend on every remote service, since a shared dependency outage could otherwise trigger repeated restarts of healthy application processes.',
    'An instance warming its local state is alive but not ready. Remove it from new traffic until initialization finishes. If optional recommendations fail, keep checkout readiness if checkout remains correct. Drain traffic before termination instead of killing in-flight requests immediately.',
    'Deep checks can overload dependencies or fail together and remove all capacity. Very shallow checks can route traffic to broken instances. Test startup, partial failure and graceful shutdown, and compare check results with synthetic or real user outcomes. Separate a dependency incident from a process that genuinely needs restart.',
    'I define health checks by the action they drive. Liveness indicates a local condition where restart may help, while readiness controls whether an instance should receive its intended work. I keep checks bounded and avoid optional-dependency cascades. I test warm-up, dependency loss and shutdown, then validate user outcomes because a green endpoint is only a signal, not proof of end-to-end availability.',
    'Should database failure make every instance fail liveness?', 'Usually not merely because the database is unavailable. Restarting all instances may add load without repairing it. Decide readiness and degradation from the required operation, while reserving liveness for an appropriate local recovery condition.', ['balancing', 'overload']),
  lesson('Failover',
    'Failover changes service or data ownership to a replacement after a failure. A stateless instance replacement differs from promoting a database writer or moving an entire region. The design must specify which state is available and who is allowed to accept work afterward.',
    'Detect failure, choose an eligible replacement, establish exclusive authority, update routing and verify the user contract. Replication position and fencing matter for stateful promotion. Fencing prevents a recovered old owner from continuing to write after ownership has moved.',
    'Before promoting a database replica, determine how much acknowledged data it has and whether loss is permitted. Prevent the previous primary from accepting writes, redirect clients and verify both a write and a subsequent read. Document how clients handle uncertain outcomes during the switch.',
    'A network partition can make a healthy owner look dead from another location. Promoting without fencing risks split-brain writes. Failback is another migration, not simply reversing a DNS record. Test stale clients, lagging replicas, repeated promotion requests and loss of the coordination service.',
    'I design failover as an ownership transition with explicit detection, eligibility, fencing, routing and verification. For stateful systems I state the possible data loss and the replication position required before promotion. I test partitions and recovered old owners, not just process termination. Recovery is complete when correct user operations succeed under one legitimate authority, not when a replacement server starts.',
    'Why is detecting a failure not enough?', 'Detection can be mistaken during a partition. A replacement must obtain legitimate exclusive authority and suitable data. Without those steps, routing to another node can create divergent writes or serve unacceptable state.', ['replicas', 'recovery']),
  lesson('Graceful degradation',
    'Graceful degradation preserves a smaller useful service when an optional capability fails. It changes the experience honestly rather than pretending the missing capability succeeded. The product must identify which functions are optional and which correctness or security requirements remain mandatory.',
    'Separate critical and optional dependencies. Apply bounded timeouts and resource isolation to optional work, then select a defined fallback such as hiding recommendations or showing explicitly stale public information. Do not bypass authorization, invent inventory availability or claim an unconfirmed payment completed.',
    'If product recommendations fail, checkout can omit that panel while still validating price, stock and payment. Tell the user when a feature is unavailable if it affects their decision. Measure successful purchases and fallback frequency, not just whether the page still renders.',
    'A fallback can overload the primary database or become a permanently neglected alternate product. Cached responses need freshness and tenant rules. Test extended degradation, restoration, missing fallback data and security boundaries; the smaller service must still satisfy its explicitly reduced contract.',
    'I design degradation around a ranked set of user capabilities. Optional work has bounded resource use, and its failure triggers an honest, tested fallback. Essential correctness and authorization do not become optional. I measure the reduced user outcome and recovery, including fallback load and stale-data limits, so a cosmetic response does not hide a failed business operation.',
    'Can stale data always be used as a fallback?', 'No. A stale public article may be acceptable, while stale account permissions or inventory commitments can be unsafe. State which data may be stale, by how much, and what to do when that bound cannot be met.', ['overload', 'authz']),
  lesson('Disaster recovery',
    'Disaster recovery restores service and data after a severe event such as regional loss, corruption or compromised infrastructure. Recovery time objective, or RTO, is the targeted restoration duration. Recovery point objective, or RPO, expresses the targeted amount of data loss measured in time.',
    'Choose backups, replication, infrastructure reconstruction and access arrangements from the required objectives. Keep recoverable copies protected from the same failure or compromised credentials. A written plan and successful backup creation do not establish that restoration will work; recovery must be exercised.',
    'Assume an RTO of one hour and an RPO of fifteen minutes. Restore a clean isolated environment from protected backups, recover permitted subsequent changes and verify business records before routing users. Measure the real elapsed recovery and actual recovered point against those targets.',
    'Replication can copy corruption, and encryption keys or identity services can become recovery dependencies. Test deleted data, unavailable regions, missing secrets and delayed detection. Validate application invariants after restore and rehearse return to normal ownership. Objectives are targets supported by evidence, not guarantees inferred from a backup schedule.',
    'I define disaster recovery from acceptable service interruption and data loss, expressed as RTO and RPO. I protect recovery copies, document infrastructure and identity dependencies, and rehearse restoration in an isolated environment. I verify actual data correctness and elapsed time before reopening traffic. Replication and backup success are inputs; only a tested recovery process shows whether the objectives are achievable.',
    'Is a replica a sufficient backup?', 'Not for every failure. Accidental deletion or corruption may propagate to replicas. Recoverable history, protected copies and tested restoration are needed for failure modes that replication alone cannot address.', ['recovery']),
  lesson('Fault injection',
    'Fault injection deliberately introduces a controlled failure to test whether the system behaves as designed. The goal is evidence about user impact, containment and recovery, not simply making an exception appear in a log. Start with a specific hypothesis and a limited blast radius: the users and resources exposed to the experiment.',
    'Define the expected steady-state user outcome, the injected failure, safety limits and stop conditions. Inject delay, dropped responses, process loss or resource exhaustion in a suitable test environment. Observe whether deadlines, isolation, alerts and recovery follow the intended contract.',
    'Delay an optional recommendation dependency for a small test cohort. Expect checkout to remain within its target and recommendations to degrade honestly. Abort if critical errors exceed a defined threshold. Remove the fault and verify recovery rather than stopping after observing breaker activation.',
    'Staging behavior may not capture production skew, but production experiments require stronger safety controls and ownership. Test ambiguous outcomes such as a response lost after commit, not only clean pre-commit failures. Record what the experiment establishes and which failure combinations remain untested.',
    'I inject faults to test a falsifiable reliability claim. I state the user outcome that should remain true, limit exposure, define abort conditions and observe both containment and recovery. I include timing and commit-boundary failures where relevant. The result is evidence for the tested scenario, not proof that every outage is safe or that a passing staging experiment guarantees production behavior.',
    'Does killing one server prove regional resilience?', 'No. Regional loss can remove data, network paths, coordination and identity dependencies together. Match the experiment to the failure model and clearly state what a single-instance test does and does not cover.', ['recovery', 'monitoring']),
  lesson('Observability',
    'Observability helps explain a system’s internal behavior from its emitted signals and user outcomes. Logs describe events, metrics summarize measurements, and traces connect work across boundaries. Collecting large amounts of telemetry is not enough if it cannot answer useful diagnostic questions.',
    'Instrument meaningful operations with consistent identifiers and timings. Correlate a user symptom with request traces, resource metrics and relevant events. Define sampling and retention, and avoid unbounded metric labels such as arbitrary user IDs. Redact secrets and sensitive payloads before export.',
    'For a slow checkout, compare user-visible latency with a trace showing database wait, a connection-pool saturation metric and a deployment event. A correlation suggests an investigation; confirm it with a bounded experiment rather than declaring the most visible graph to be the root cause.',
    'Sampling can miss rare failures, clocks can distort cross-host timelines, and telemetry pipelines can fail during overload. Preserve enough context to investigate while controlling cost and sensitive data. Test alert usefulness, missing-signal behavior and whether an operator can locate the affected tenant without leaking their data.',
    'I design observability around questions about user impact and cause. Metrics reveal trends and saturation, traces locate time across a request, and logs explain specific events. I correlate them with safe identifiers, control label cardinality (the number of distinct metric-label combinations) and sampling, and protect sensitive data. I verify that operators can use the evidence to choose and confirm a mitigation rather than equating dashboard volume with understanding.',
    'Is correlation enough to establish root cause?', 'No. A deployment and an error spike can coincide without a causal relationship. Use traces, contrasting cohorts, configuration evidence or a safe reversible experiment to test the suspected mechanism.', ['signals', 'monitoring']),
  lesson('API gateway',
    'An API gateway is an entry boundary that can route requests and apply shared edge policies. It may handle credential validation, rate limits or response composition. It does not automatically understand the domain rules for every resource behind it.',
    'Match a request to a destination, establish trusted caller context and enforce bounded admission and timeouts. Strip or overwrite untrusted identity headers. The resource-owning service still checks whether the authenticated principal may perform the requested action on the actual tenant and resource.',
    'A gateway validates a customer’s token and routes an order lookup. The order service loads the order within the trusted tenant scope and authorizes ownership. A valid token must not permit replacing the order ID with another customer’s ID to retrieve their record.',
    'A gateway can become a shared availability bottleneck, while aggregating calls can amplify latency or traffic. Edge retries may repeat writes. Test bypass paths, stale policy caches, request size limits and identity forwarding. Avoid moving every business decision into a central layer that services can accidentally circumvent.',
    'I use a gateway for common ingress concerns such as routing, identity validation and admission control. It establishes trusted context, but each owning service enforces resource-level authorization and business invariants. I bound gateway work and account for its availability and retry behavior. I test direct-service and malformed-header paths so the gateway improves the boundary without becoming the only place that protects data.',
    'Why authorize again inside the service?', 'The service owns the current resource and business state, which the gateway may not know. It also protects internal or bypass callers. Authentication at ingress is not permission to access every downstream object.', ['authz', 'balancing']),
  lesson('Authentication',
    'Authentication establishes who or what is calling. It may involve a user login, a service credential or a signed token. It is distinct from authorization: proving an identity does not establish that the identity may read or modify a particular resource.',
    'Use an appropriate identity provider or authentication mechanism, validate credentials or token signatures and required claims, and establish a session with a defined lifetime. Issuer, audience and expiry checks matter. Session cookies and bearer tokens have different storage, revocation and browser-security considerations.',
    'An API receives a signed access token intended for a different service. Even if the signature is valid, reject the wrong audience. After login, keep session transport protected and do not log the raw token. Test expiry, logout, revoked sessions and compromised credentials.',
    'Key rotation, account recovery and session theft are part of the lifecycle. Multi-factor authentication reduces some risks but does not replace session protection. Avoid custom cryptographic protocols, define reauthentication for sensitive actions, and ensure error messages and recovery flows do not disclose unnecessary account information.',
    'Authentication establishes a trusted caller identity using a suitable provider or credential mechanism. I validate the full credential context, including issuer, audience and lifetime where applicable, then protect session storage and transport. I design expiry, revocation, key rotation and recovery as first-class behavior. Authorization remains a separate check on each sensitive action; a successfully signed-in user is not automatically entitled to every record.',
    'Is verifying a token signature sufficient?', 'No. A correctly signed token can be expired, issued by an untrusted authority or intended for another audience. Validate the claims and lifecycle required by the chosen protocol and service.', ['authn']),
  lesson('Authorization',
    'Authorization decides whether a principal may perform an action on a particular resource in its current context. A role can contribute to that decision, but identity, tenant, ownership and resource state may also matter. The decision must protect the actual operation, not just hide a button.',
    'At the owning boundary, load or address the resource within a trusted scope, evaluate the policy and enforce it before returning data or changing state. Deny by default where appropriate. Protect alternate endpoints, bulk operations and background jobs under the same relevant rules.',
    'A manager may approve an expense only for their own department while it is pending. Changing the URL’s expense ID or replaying a request after approval must not bypass those rules. Test both a forbidden resource and a once-valid action whose state has changed.',
    'Checking permission and changing state separately can create a time-of-check/time-of-use race. Cache lifetimes can delay revocation. Combine authorization with the relevant atomic state transition, and test tenant changes, deleted resources, bulk IDs and stale privileges. Return safe errors without exposing another customer’s data.',
    'I authorize at the resource-owning boundary using the authenticated principal, requested action, tenant and current resource state. I enforce least privilege and test alternate paths rather than relying on UI visibility. For state-changing operations I protect the check and transition against races. I also define policy-cache freshness and revocation behavior, since yesterday’s permission or a valid login is not proof of permission now.',
    'Why is checking a role insufficient for object access?', 'A role may permit an operation category without permitting every object. A support user for tenant A must not access tenant B’s records merely because both use the same role name. Evaluate the resource scope too.', ['authz']),
  lesson('Encryption and secrets',
    'Encryption protects data under a cryptographic key; secret management protects credentials and key material throughout their lifecycle. Transport encryption and storage encryption address different exposure paths. Neither makes an authorized application incapable of leaking plaintext through logs or incorrect access decisions.',
    'Use authenticated transport, supported storage encryption and a managed approach to key access. Applications should retrieve secret references through controlled identities rather than embed credentials in source or logs. Define rotation, revocation, auditing and recovery for the systems that can decrypt or use the secret.',
    'A service authenticates to a secret manager using its workload identity and retrieves a scoped database credential. Rotate that credential with a safe overlap and verify new connections before revoking the old one. Redact it from errors, traces, crash reports and support exports.',
    'An attacker with application identity may still request decryption, so key permissions and data authorization remain essential. Lost keys can make backups unrecoverable. Test rotation during traffic, revocation, secret-manager outage and restoration. Distinguish reversible encryption from password hashing rather than storing recoverable passwords as a convenience.',
    'I protect sensitive data in transit and at rest, and separately control who can obtain keys or credentials. I keep secrets out of source, logs and user responses, use scoped workload access, and design rotation and revocation. I test recovery dependencies as well as normal retrieval. Encryption reduces specific exposures; it does not replace authorization, safe logging or a plan for compromised application identities.',
    'Does encryption at rest prevent an application from exposing data?', 'No. The application may legitimately decrypt data and then return or log it incorrectly. Authorization, tenant isolation and redaction still protect what happens after decryption.', ['secrets', 'authn']),
  lesson('Multi-tenancy',
    'Multi-tenancy lets one product serve several customer organizations while keeping their data and permitted actions isolated. Sharing infrastructure does not make their information interchangeable. Isolation applies to records, caches, messages, exports, metrics and operator access, not just database queries.',
    'Derive tenant scope from trusted identity and membership, then carry it through every resource lookup and background action. Choose shared tables, separate schemas or separate stores based on requirements. Include tenant identity in relevant keys and policies, while controlling noisy-neighbor resource use separately.',
    'A user from tenant A requests document 42 while tenant B also has document 42. The lookup must use both the trusted tenant and document identity, and the cache must preserve that distinction. Repeat the test through search, bulk export and a background-job replay.',
    'A missing scope in one join or cache can defeat otherwise careful isolation. Tenant reassignment and deleted membership complicate queued work. Test forged headers, cross-tenant IDs, support tooling, stale membership and per-tenant quotas. Physical separation can reduce some risks but still requires correct authorization and operations.',
    'I treat tenant identity as an end-to-end security boundary. I establish it from trusted membership, scope every resource operation and preserve it in caches, events and background work. I choose the storage isolation model from the required risk and operational trade-offs, then test cross-tenant access through every alternate path. I also enforce resource fairness because data isolation alone does not prevent noisy-neighbor outages.',
    'Can the client choose a tenant by sending a header?', 'A client may request a tenant context, but the server must validate that choice against trusted identity and membership. Treating an arbitrary header as authority allows one customer to select another customer’s scope.', ['tenant']),
  lesson('Schema evolution',
    'Schema evolution changes the shape or meaning of stored data, APIs or events while callers and historical records still exist. A safe change considers old producers, new consumers, old consumers and new producers together. Compatibility includes semantics and defaults, not only whether JSON parses.',
    'Prefer an expand-and-contract sequence where supported: add compatible fields or structures, deploy readers that accept both forms, move writers, backfill historical data, verify adoption and remove the old form only after its users are gone. Explicitly check each protocol’s unknown-field and required-field behavior.',
    'Replace a single address string with structured address fields. First allow readers to accept either representation, then update writers and backfill. Define what happens for old records without a postal code. Do not immediately make the new field mandatory while older clients still send the old shape.',
    'Changing units, enum meanings or null behavior can break consumers even without renaming a field. Replayed events may use much older versions. Test a producer/consumer compatibility matrix, historical fixtures and rollback. Renaming or deleting a field is not automatically safe because the current application compiled successfully.',
    'I evolve schemas through a compatibility plan that covers deployed clients and historical data. I separate adding support, switching writers, backfilling and removing the old representation. I define defaults, null handling, unknown fields and semantic meaning, then test old/new combinations and replay. I verify actual adoption before contraction so rollback or delayed consumers do not encounter a representation they cannot understand.',
    'Is adding a field always backward compatible?', 'No. Strict consumers may reject unknown fields, signatures may cover an exact representation, or a new required meaning may change behavior. Compatibility depends on the protocol, validation rules and semantics of both sides.', ['kafka', 'canary']),
  lesson('Data migration',
    'A data migration moves or transforms existing records while preserving the business facts they represent. It must cover historical data and writes that occur while copying. A successful copy command is not proof that the new system is complete, current or safe to serve.',
    'Define source authority, transformation rules, a snapshot boundary and capture of subsequent changes. Backfill in bounded, restartable units with stable identities. Compare invariants and representative reads, then perform a controlled ownership cutover. Retain a feasible rollback or forward-repair path for each phase.',
    'Move orders to a new store by copying a consistent starting dataset and applying later updates in a known order. Compare counts and monetary totals by tenant, inspect mismatches, and verify deletes as well as inserts. Switch writes only when ownership and catch-up criteria are satisfied.',
    'Dual writes can partially succeed and diverge. A delete during backfill can be resurrected by a stale copy. Test concurrent updates, retries, duplicates, out-of-order changes and partial cutover. State when rollback stops being simple because new writes or transformations cannot be represented by the old system.',
    'I treat migration as a data-and-ownership transition. I specify the snapshot and change-capture boundary, make copying restartable, and verify domain invariants before switching authority. I explicitly handle updates and deletes during the move and test partial failure. Cutover has measurable entry criteria and a rollback or repair strategy; row-count equality alone does not prove that the migrated business state is correct.',
    'Why are matching row counts insufficient?', 'Two datasets can have equal counts but different values, missing updates or incorrectly retained deleted records. Compare meaningful keys, checksums where appropriate and domain invariants such as balances or order states.', ['replicas', 'partitions']),
  lesson('Canary deployment',
    'A canary deployment exposes a new version to a limited, observable portion of traffic before broader rollout. It reduces the initial impact of a bad change. A canary needs a meaningful comparison and stop policy; deploying to one server without measuring outcomes is not enough.',
    'Choose a representative cohort and compare user outcomes with a suitable baseline. Gate expansion on correctness, errors, latency and relevant business signals, with minimum sample and observation requirements. Define who can stop the rollout and how the previous version or safe mode is restored.',
    'Route 1% of eligible search traffic to a new ranking service while comparing failures and latency with the baseline. Include different query types and regions. If a guardrail fails, stop expansion and restore routing. The percentage is illustrative; rare operations may need targeted tests and longer observation.',
    'A tiny cohort may miss rare failures or exclude heavy customers. Shared database changes can affect the baseline too, and rollback may not undo changed data. Test mixed versions and schema compatibility. Avoid promoting automatically on a small clean sample that cannot detect the expected failure rate.',
    'I use a canary to limit exposure while collecting evidence for a release. I choose a representative cohort, define success and abort signals, and establish enough observation to make those signals meaningful. I test compatibility with the baseline and keep a feasible rollback or repair plan. A clean canary reduces uncertainty; it does not prove safety for untested customers, traffic patterns or data transitions.',
    'Does rolling back code always reverse a bad release?', 'No. The release may already have changed data, sent events or triggered external effects. Compatibility, reversible transitions and a forward-repair plan are required when restoring the old binary cannot restore the old state.', ['canary']),
  lesson('Cost model',
    'A cost model explains how workload and architectural choices create spending. It includes compute, storage, requests, network transfer and operational effort. The cheapest component price is not necessarily the cheapest way to deliver a correct user outcome at the required reliability.',
    'Estimate each cost driver from measured or stated assumptions, then relate it to useful units such as successful orders. Include replicas, indexes, retention, background work and failure headroom. Compare alternatives at representative peak and ordinary traffic, and revisit the model as usage changes.',
    'A cache may reduce database reads but add cache infrastructure and invalidation work. A cross-region design may improve a recovery objective while adding replication and transfer costs. Compare cost per successful operation and the reliability benefit rather than counting only servers removed.',
    'Retries, high-cardinality telemetry, abandoned jobs and data egress can become hidden multipliers. Aggressive cost cuts can remove the capacity needed during failure or recovery. Test the operational consequences of reducing redundancy or retention, and separate variable usage costs from fixed commitments and engineering maintenance.',
    'I model cost from the workload and required service contract. I estimate compute, data, requests, transfer and operational effort, then compare cost per useful completed outcome. I include redundancy and recovery capacity instead of assuming steady-state minimum resources are sufficient. I validate major assumptions with usage measurements and explain what reliability, latency or maintenance trade-off each saving introduces.',
    'Why not always maximize machine utilization?', 'Very high utilization can leave no room for bursts, deployments or a failed peer’s traffic and can increase queueing. Optimize the cost of meeting the service target, including recovery headroom, rather than utilization in isolation.', ['cost', 'overload']),
  lesson('Runbook',
    'A runbook gives an operator an owned, actionable response to a specific operational condition. It connects a symptom to evidence, safe mitigation and verification. A list of commands without prerequisites or expected outcomes can make an incident worse instead of helping recovery.',
    'State the alert and user impact, required access, diagnostic steps, decision points, bounded actions and escalation owner. Include stop conditions and how to confirm success or undo a change. Prefer commands and links whose assumptions are explicit, with secrets kept out of the document.',
    'For rising queue age, first inspect consumer health, downstream errors and saturation. If the dependency is failing, blindly increasing workers may amplify load. The runbook should distinguish safe intake reduction from justified capacity changes and verify that oldest-job age and user completion recover afterward.',
    'Runbooks drift as dashboards, permissions and architecture change. Rehearse them with a person who did not write them and record the result. Test missing access, misleading alerts and failed mitigation. Automation should retain guardrails and ownership rather than executing destructive steps whenever one metric changes.',
    'I write a runbook around a user-impacting symptom and a sequence of evidence-based decisions. It specifies prerequisites, safe bounded actions, stop conditions, verification, rollback and escalation. I test it through rehearsal and maintain its links and access requirements. The objective is repeatable recovery by an operator under pressure, not a long document or an unreviewed script that assumes every incident has the same cause.',
    'How do you know a runbook is useful?', 'Have another operator use it in a controlled exercise and measure whether they can diagnose, mitigate and verify recovery without hidden knowledge. Fix missing prerequisites, ambiguous decisions and unsafe steps revealed by that rehearsal.', ['onCall']),
]);
